        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Fuel is the whole game.</strong> Run the tank and the carburettor bowl completely dry, or fill it, stabilize it and run the engine five minutes so the treated fuel reaches everything. Leaving half a tank of untreated gasoline is what fills repair shops in April.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            After that, change the oil warm, scrape the deck, sharpen and balance the blade, fit a fresh plug and a clean filter, and fog the cylinder if it is sitting more than four months. Battery machines skip the fuel work but need the pack stored indoors at 40 to 60 percent charge.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The whole routine is about an hour, and it is the difference between a mower that starts on the second pull in spring and one that needs a carburettor before it cuts a single blade of grass.
